      "math_academy_id": "q-87102",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/edb2f75e0e5ba2d16afde5e335b8adc888a621d7e24720fab45a397bc6fb497d.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=4f(x)+2?$",
      "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=4f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=4f(x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/231d9ba40de9b7a061dd56be38b4145f3fd11b8488535957b8317c7c0e3c1cef.png)\n\n- Then, translate it up by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=4f(x)+2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14215498/assets/95fce5c1cbcf16e036d4fe86a526c1910acd2b49dec8ac31e1641b137ef929df.png)",
